  "console-over-management": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "Figure 1-1 points callout 5, the console port, at the upper jack of the stacked pair and callout 3, the management port, at the lower; Figure 1-7 puts the MGT lamp under the stack. The management jack takes the bare part because its lamp is that one on the bottom row, not a pair in the bezel."
  },
  "gate-1": {
   "confidence": "measured",
   "note": "BOTH DATA-SHEET PHOTOGRAPHS PASS, AT LOW RESOLUTION. The front, Figure 1, is 907 x 217 px with the face 827 px across and 84 tall: 9.85 against the stated 444.4 / 44.45 = 10.0, 1.5% out. The rear, Figure 2, is 853 px by 84: 10.15, 1.5% the other way. Scaled on the stated width the front is 1.861 px per mm, AND THE RJ-45 PITCH THEN READS 13.97 mm - 26.0 px, averaged over the 20 steps inside the four blocks of one row - which is the 0.550 in ganged-jack pitch the registry's rj45-ganged notes record from a TE 2x6 magjack, on a quantity the scale never saw. The registry states that pitch in its notes and not as a key, so the block gives 13.97 as a number; the 12.7 part leaves 1.27 mm of wall between openings, as the photograph shows. The SFP+ pair steps 14.5 against the registry's 14.25. So both axes are read off the photographs, and at under 2 px per mm every position is good to about half a millimetre and no better."
  },
  "interface-names": {
   "confidence": "estimated",
   "note": "NOT IN EITHER STAGED CISCO DOCUMENT. Searched the installation guide and the data sheet for GigabitEthernet, FastEthernet and their short forms and found nothing; port naming lives in the IOS configuration guides, which are not staged. The names are the NetBox devicetype-library device type for this model, a community file (netbox-community/devicetype-library, device-types/Cisco/WS-C4948E.yaml): GigabitEthernet1/1 to 1/48, TenGigabitEthernet1/49 to 1/52 and FastEthernet1 for the management port. THAT FILE ALSO TYPES THE MANAGEMENT PORT 100BASE-TX, which its name suggests and Cisco's own documents contradict - the guide says \"a 10/100/1000 RJ-45 port Ethernet Management port\" and the data sheet \"10/100/1000BASE-TX\" - so the name is taken from it and the speed from Cisco. Estimated, because a community file is the only source for the names."
  },
  "lamps": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "Installation guide, Table 1-5 and Table 1-1. STATUS: green up and running, red system fault, flashing amber power-on self-test. Port LINK lamps: green link, amber disabled, blinking amber link fault. FAN: green tray OK, red a fan has failed. PS1 and PS2: green input power OK, red fault. The management lamp is the data sheet's \"green (good), orange (disabled), or off (not connected)\". Positions are Figure 1-7's callouts placed on the photograph: PS1, PS2 and FAN in a column at the right, MGT and STATUS on the bottom row with the recessed RESET beside STATUS."
  },
  "port-order": {
   "confidence": "photo-measured",
   "note": "Each block prints twelve lamps in one row under its six columns, alternately an up and a down triangle, with a numeral under each running 1 to 12, 13 to 24 and so on. Two lamps per column, up then down, so the odd port is the upper jack. The four uplink lamps under the SFP+ cages run the same way with 49 to 52 beneath them, on a row of their own that reads about a millimetre higher than the jack lamps' (centred 36.0 mm down against 37.1). SO A NUMERAL SITS UNDER ITS LAMP AND NOT UNDER ITS JACK: twelve lamps on a 5.8 mm step stand beneath six columns on a 13.97 step, and L61 reports the sixteen numerals that land within a millimetre or so of their jack's centre by accident. The legends printed ABOVE the jacks are not legible at this resolution and are a gap."
  },
  "power": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "Data sheet Table 2: \"Typical operating power 230W at 25C\" and \"Maximum power 275W at 55C\"; Table 1 calls the first \"230W average\". These are the switch's draw, ambient stated, configuration not. Heat is printed three ways and they are not one measure: the data sheet gives 1173 BTU per hour on AC input and 1251 on DC, and the guide's Table 1-2 gives 1364 BTU/hour \"worst case\". The guide's is carried, as the largest."
  },
  "rear-face": {
   "confidence": "photo-measured",
   "note": "The data sheet's Figure 2 at 1.919 px per mm. Supply 1 runs 8 to 230 px, the fan tray 232 to 598, supply 2 from 600 to 822, and a strip of bare chassis with an earth mark closes the face. The guide's Figure 1-2 names them in the same order: power supply 1 (primary), fan tray, power supply 2 (redundant)."
  },
  "rows": {
   "confidence": "photo-measured",
   "note": "Jack openings read 69-86 and 97-114 px down a face that runs from 53 to 137, 84 px for the stated 44.45 mm, so at 0.529 mm per px the rows centre 12.9 and 27.7 mm down: tops at 7.4 and 22.2 for an 11.0 part, a 14.8 step. The upper jacks show their keyways up and the lower their keyways down, the usual stacked mirror. The SFP+ openings centre 12.5 and 27.5, which is the registry's 15.0 belly-to-belly step, upper cage upright over the lower turned 180."
  },
  "sources": {
